# Part 1: IdentifierReference `may_have_side_effects`

The full implementation is shown in the page:

```rust
impl<'a> MayHaveSideEffects<'a> for IdentifierReference<'a> {
    fn may_have_side_effects(&self, ctx: &impl MayHaveSideEffectsContext<'a>) -> bool {
        match self.name.as_str() {
            "NaN" | "Infinity" | "undefined" => false,
            _ => {
                ctx.unknown_global_side_effects()
                    && ctx.is_global_reference(self)
                    && !is_known_global_identifier(self.name.as_str())
            }
        }
    }
}
```

It treats `"NaN" | "Infinity" | "undefined"` as side-effect-free directly, and otherwise returns true only when unknown-global side effects are enabled, the reference is a global, and it is not a known-safe global identifier.

**Regarding the helper listing safe global names:** The function `is_known_global_identifier` (and companions like `is_known_global_constructor`, `is_known_global_property`, `is_pure_global_function`) is only **imported** here from a separate module:

```rust
